#emph-block[My graduating students are on the job market for faculty positions, so I thought I would write some notes on my experience to help them.]


= Timeline

- In most cases, universities look for new faculty in the *Fall* (similarly to PhD admission). 
- To apply for a position in the Fall of year X, then you want to start applying in the Fall of year X - 1 (and the interviews typically happen in the Spring of year X).
- You can graduate in the Spring or even Summer of Fall X.
- It is possible and common to negotiate so that you can start a semseter (e.g., Spring of X+1) or even a full year (Fall X+1) later.


= Search for Opening Positions


- The traditional and standard way is go to the departmental websites of various universities and look for their openings.  E.g., go to the websites of the top 100 CS depts listed on CSRankings.  It's also possible to go to HigherEds, CRA, etc websites to look for academic postings. In recent times, people also post about academic opportunities using social media such as Linkedin or X/Twitter. 
- However, this process is manual and time consuming. Depts can post jobs anytime (e.g., sometimes very early, sometimes late because they didn't have the green light to do so until late Spring).  So we have built cspicks.roars.dev/jobs.html---a website that leverages AI agents to frequently crawl the web and collect academic job postings from the US and all around the world and include both tenure-track and teaching positions.
CSPicks only posts current job openings that are officially listed on a univeresity website so these are both official and accurate (and also does not miss any special opportunities listed in social media such as Linkedin as those often have a corresponding official university postings). It also includes information about immigration (e.g., some posting explicitly mention they do not sponsor international applicants).      


- In many cases advisors post on social media or mention at conferences that their students are graduating and their colleagues can tag or contact them advertise about their departmental openings. This is a type of "referral" and can help the students as the advisors are using their reputations to vouch for their (still unknown) students.

  
